const featureDocs=[
 ['forces','Wind, drag, attraction and swirl','Dynamics','Open Forces, collisions & lifetime. Wind X/Y/Z adds acceleration in centimeters per second squared. Air resistance slows velocity exponentially per second. Positive Pull / push attracts particles to Force center; negative pushes away. Swirl force adds tangential acceleration around the vertical axis through Force center; its sign reverses direction. Swirl lift adds vertical acceleration. These forces combine with aimed or preset movement, gravity and turbulence. Locked ring motion ignores forces and collisions. Force values can be animated through Change a property. Recipe buttons add independent layers and preserve your current effect.','Add tornado, then increase Air resistance to make the swirl settle.'],
 ['surface','Collision surface','Dynamics','Enable Collide with a surface, then choose Bounce or Remove particle in Forces, collisions & lifetime. Collision floor height places a horizontal plane in effect-local centimeters. Bounciness controls retained vertical speed (0 to 1). Surface friction reduces sideways speed per impact (0 to 1). Remove particle deletes a particle when it crosses below the plane. Locked ring movement ignores collisions. These settings export to Niagara and baked Blender animation. This surface is not arbitrary Unreal scene geometry.','Use high bounciness and low friction for ricochet sparks.'],
 ['lifetime','Particle spin and color over lifetime','Dynamics','Particle spin rotates each sprite or mesh continuously in degrees per second; negative spins in reverse. Spin is independent of the direction particles travel and can be animated. Enable Color over lifetime and choose Fade toward to blend from the layer color to a final color based on each particle’s own age. Layer color animation still works, and the result blends in linear color space. Projects, presets, Niagara export and Blender baking preserve these controls.','Fade hot yellow sparks toward red while spinning them at 180 degrees per second.'],
 ['anime','Anime filter','Appearance','Enable Anime filter under Look & feel for the selected layer. Sprites gain sharp silhouettes, flat shading bands, and dark ink edges. Imported meshes use view-based stepped shading and an ink rim. Shading steps ranges from 2 to 6; fewer steps give a flatter cel look. Ink outline ranges from 0 to 25 percent; set 0 for no ink. Glow boosts the brightness of anime shading and trails while keeping ink edges dark. Turn Glow off for a flatter look. Unreal scene bloom controls the exported halo. Particle motion and animation stay unchanged. Each layer can use a different style. Apply this look to all layers copies the current filter switch, shading steps and outline across the whole effect. With the switch off, it removes the filter from every layer. The settings save with projects and presets and export into generated Niagara materials and Blender materials. Blender uses view-based mesh shading, so its look differs from camera-facing sprites. Native project systems are not changed by the builder filter.','Try Campfire with 3 shading steps and 8 percent outline, then add a softer smoke layer.'],
 ['nativeWorkspace','Project Niagara workspace','Native editor','Choose Project Niagara systems to browse your connected project. Save and close that Unreal project before reading assets or saving a copy. Read project effects lists native Niagara systems. Select a system to see its emitters, CPU or GPU simulation, renderer types, and exposed User Parameters. Float, integer, boolean, vector and linear RGBA color parameters can be changed here. Save a new system duplicates the whole native asset and applies those controls without replacing its graphs. The source stays unchanged. Other parameter types remain editable in Unreal. Open full editor & preview launches Unreal with the selected system. Native GPU, skeletal, event, collision, ribbon, stage and script features execute in Unreal, not in the Ember particle preview. Builder projects and native systems remain separate; the native workspace does not translate graphs into builder layers.','Expose User Parameters in a complex Niagara effect to give it a small set of easy controls.'],
 ['nativeCopies','Save native system copies','Native editor','Copies are saved under Content/Ember/Systems with unique names. Use letters, numbers and underscores. A copy retains the full Niagara system and references to its existing dependencies. It belongs to the connected project; Ember JSON saves and presets do not include it. Do not move the system to another project without its dependencies. Close Unreal before saving a copy, then open the new system to preview and continue editing.','Make Fireball_Blue from an existing native fireball and adjust its exposed color.'],
 ['start','Getting started','Workflow','Choose a preset or Blank particle. Adjust the selected layer on the right, then add more layers on the left. Save an Ember project to retain editable settings.','Try combining Campfire with Soft smoke.'],
 ['presets','24 starting effects','Workflow','Presets supply editable starting values. Selecting a preset replaces the selected layer only. Add to effect creates another particle layer and keeps your existing layers. Blank particle is a simple starting point. Use the search box and Energy / Nature filters.','A preset is a starting point; it is not a locked effect.'],
 ['myPresets','Create your own presets','Workflow','Click Save as preset in the library. Name it and choose Whole effect to keep all layers, or Selected particle layer to save just the layer you are editing. Particle positions, motion, randomness, and animation are included. Find saved copies in My presets or search by name. Clicking a saved preset replaces only the active layer’s particle settings and animation. Other layers, the project name, and the active layer’s name and visibility stay as they are. For a preset with multiple layers, choose which particle layer to use, or click Add all layers to effect in the picker to add the entire preset alongside your current layers. Undo restores the previous settings. Add to effect combines its layers with your current effect when there is room within the eight-layer limit. The × button deletes the library copy after confirmation. Presets save immediately on this device and remain available after closing and reopening Ember. Save an Ember project file for a portable backup. Saving a preset makes a copy: later edits do not change it. Save again to create another version. Up to 100 saved presets.','Build a fireball from flames, smoke, and sparks, then save the whole effect as Fireball.'],
 ['layers','Particle layers','Layers','An effect can contain up to eight independent emitters. Select a layer to edit its controls. Each has its own color, spawn shape, motion, lifetime, and particle appearance.','Combine a continuous smoke layer with a burst of sparks.'],
 ['addPreset','Add preset particles','Layers','Click Add to effect underneath any preset to add its particles as a new layer. Existing layers and their settings are preserved. The new layer is selected so you can change its amount, color, position, motion, and animation. Each layer is independent, and all visible layers export together to Unreal. The limit is eight layers; remove a layer to make room. Undo removes an addition and Redo brings it back.','Try adding Soft smoke to Campfire, then move the smoke layer above the flames.'],
 ['add','Add a layer','Layers','The + button creates a new Blank particle layer and selects it. Existing layers keep running. The limit is eight layers.','Create a separate layer when you need another color or behavior.'],
 ['duplicate','Duplicate a layer','Layers','The duplicate button copies the selected layer’s settings. The copy has its own controls and simulation.','Duplicate sparks, then change the copy to blue and slow it down.'],
 ['visibility','Show and hide layers','Layers','The checkbox beside a layer controls whether it appears in the preview and export. Hidden layers keep their settings. They do not advance in the preview while hidden.','Hide other layers to isolate the one you are editing.'],
 ['remove','Remove a layer','Layers','The × button removes that layer. At least one layer remains. Undo can restore a removed layer during this app session.','Save your project before making large changes.'],
 ['rename','Layer names','Layers','Edit the name beside the layer list to describe its role. Names are saved in the project.','Use names such as Core fire, Smoke plume, and Impact sparks.'],
 ['color','Particle color','Appearance','Choose a color swatch or use the custom color picker. The selected layer updates immediately; existing particles also receive its color.','Warm colors suit embers; low-saturation colors suit smoke.'],
 ['amount','Particle amount','Appearance','In Continuous flow, this is the number of particles emitted per second (5–200). Burst creates twice this amount at once. More layers can increase the total density.','Begin around 30 and increase until the effect reads clearly.'],
 ['size','Particle size','Appearance','Controls the starting size of each particle (1–30). Individual particles vary slightly. Glow also adds a visible halo.','Large particles need fewer spawns to fill space.'],
 ['sprite','Particle appearance','Appearance','Choose Soft dot, Star, Square, Diamond, or Ring / bubble. These are procedural particle masks, not imported 3D meshes.','Use squares for confetti and rings for bubbles.'],
 ['glow','Glow','Appearance','Adds a bright core and halo in the preview. In Unreal it uses emissive particle color; scene bloom affects the visible halo.','Turn glow off for leaves, confetti, and opaque-looking debris.'],
 ['trailLength','Trail length','Appearance','Enable Trail, then adjust Trail length from 0.1× to 10×. 1× preserves the original streak length, 3× triples it, and 10× makes a much longer streak. Each layer has its own length. Trail length can change over time using a property animation, and is saved in projects and presets. Preview streaks extend behind the particle along its current velocity; they do not record a curved movement history. Niagara export draws a separate fading streak behind each particle using its current velocity and the same length multiplier. Re-export older Unreal effects to get this trail renderer.','Try a laser or spark effect with Trail on and Trail length at 4×.'],
 ['trail','Trails','Appearance','Draws a fading streak behind moving particles. Unreal export adds a separate velocity-aligned trail renderer and fading material, keeping the main particle shape intact. Use Trail length to extend it. Re-export older Unreal effects to get the corrected renderer.','Increase movement speed to make trails easier to see.'],
 ['endSize','Size over lifetime','Lifetime','Size at end of life is a multiplier from 0–5. 0 shrinks to nothing; 1 holds the size; 3 grows to three times the starting size. The change is linear.','Growing particles work well for steam and smoke.'],
 ['endOpacity','Opacity over lifetime','Lifetime','Opacity at end of life ranges from 0 (transparent) to 1 (visible). Particles fade in briefly, then change linearly toward this value.','Use 0 to avoid particles disappearing abruptly.'],
 ['life','Lifetime','Lifetime','Each particle lives for 50–100% of this value (1–8 seconds). Short lifetimes produce quick effects; long lifetimes allow wider motion.','Increase lifetime before reducing speed for drifting effects.'],
 ['mode','Continuous and burst emission','Motion','Continuous flow emits particles steadily. Burst emits them together. The preview repeats bursts after Lifetime plus 0.5 seconds. Playback speed affects preview timing only.','Use Burst for explosions and Continuous flow for weather.'],
 ['shape','Spawn shapes','Motion','Point concentrates the origin. Sphere fills a volume. Cone spreads moving particles upward. Ring places particles around a circle. Box spreads them across an area. Some presets have specialized directions.','Use Sphere for explosions, Box for weather, and Cone for fountains.'],
 ['speed','Movement speed','Motion','Sets the initial particle velocity (1–100). Each preset interprets direction differently. Gravity and turbulence change velocity after spawning.','High speed plus a short lifetime produces tight impacts.'],
 ['spread','Spread','Motion','Widens the spawn region or velocity spread (5–100), depending on the selected spawn shape. Changing it affects new particles.','Use a low value for a narrow jet and a high value for a shower.'],
 ['positionX','Starting position X','Motion','Moves this layer’s spawn region left or right along X. Enter an offset from −10000 to 10000 in Unreal units (centimeters). Other layers keep their own positions.','Use Center to reset all three offsets.'],
 ['positionY','Starting position Y','Motion','Moves this layer’s spawn region forward or back along Y. Positions are local to the effect and are included in saved projects and Unreal exports.','Separate smoke and sparks by giving each layer a different offset.'],
 ['positionZ','Starting position Z','Motion','Moves this layer’s spawn region up or down along Z, relative to the preset’s original starting region. Editing a position restarts that layer so you see the new placement immediately. Ground bounce stays at Z=0.','Raise a fireball with a positive Z offset.'],
 ['emitScaleX','Emission stretch X','Motion','Scales the spawn area left and right along X, from 0.1× to 10×. One keeps the original width. Two doubles it. Starting-position offsets stay separate. For ring orbit, X and Y stretch make an oval while particles follow the oval path.','Try X=3 and Y=0.5 for a long, narrow emission area.'],
 ['emitScaleY','Emission stretch Y','Motion','Scales spawn depth along Y from 0.1× to 10×. This affects the emission area rather than the size of individual particles. Reset stretch restores X, Y, and Z to 1×.','Use a box shape and increase Y to fill a long corridor.'],
 ['emitScaleZ','Emission stretch Z','Motion','Scales the spawn height around its original center, from 0.1× to 10×. A sphere becomes taller or flatter. Point, cone, box, and horizontal ring regions gain height variation when Z is above 1×. Orbit particles keep their individual starting heights.','Increase Z above 1 to turn a flat starting region into a taller band.'],
 ['radiusVariation','Distance from center variation','Motion','Gives each new ring particle a randomly chosen distance from the center, from 0–100% variation. At zero, particles start at the same radius. At 40%, radii range from 60% to 140% of the base ring radius. Orbiting particles keep their chosen orbit size as they spin. Works with stretched oval rings and ordinary ring emission. Animation keys affect newly created particles.','Try 35% to turn a thin ring into a wider band.'],
 ['spinVariation','Random ring rotation speed','Motion','Spin variation sets a stable random speed difference for each orbiting particle, from 0–100%. Zero keeps the same speed for all particles. At 50%, speeds range from half to one-and-a-half times Ring spin. At 100%, they range from zero to twice Ring spin. The random choice stays fixed for each particle and can be adjusted with animation keys.','Try Ring spin=90 and Spin variation=35 for a less uniform ring.'],
 ['directionRandom','Random launch direction','Motion','Turns each new particle’s velocity by a random heading and elevation offset, up to the selected number of degrees (0–180). Speed is preserved. This works with preset and aimed movement; ring orbit keeps its tangent direction and uses Spin variation instead. Changes affect new particles.','Use 10 degrees to loosen a focused jet without scattering it everywhere.'],
 ['spriteRotationRandom','Random particle angle','Appearance','Gives new particles a random starting visual angle between minus and plus the selected number of degrees (0–180). Use stars, squares, or diamonds to see the difference. Soft dots and round rings look the same when rotated. This changes appearance rather than the travel direction.','Choose Square and set Random particle angle to 180 for varied confetti orientations.'],
 ['motion','Velocity direction and ring motion','Motion','Choose Preset movement for the original effect, Aim the velocity to send particles in a chosen direction, or Spin around a ring to keep particles on a horizontal orbit. Try a spinning ring sets up the selected layer. Orbit uses Ring spin instead of Movement speed, gravity, or turbulence.','Use Spin around a ring, then set Ring spin to −90 to reverse it.'],
 ['velocityYaw','Velocity heading','Motion','In Aim the velocity mode, heading turns the starting velocity around the upward Z axis. Zero points along +X, 90 along +Y, and −90 along −Y. Values range from −180 to 180 degrees. Heading keys apply to newly created particles.','Use heading 0 and elevation 0 for a horizontal jet along X.'],
 ['velocityPitch','Velocity elevation','Motion','In Aim the velocity mode, elevation tilts starting velocity above or below the horizontal plane. Zero is horizontal, 90 points straight up, and −90 points down. Movement speed controls how fast particles travel. Elevation keys apply to new particles.','Use elevation 45 to launch particles diagonally upward.'],
 ['ringSpin','Ring spin','Motion','Spin around a ring keeps particles on a horizontal orbit around the layer’s starting position. Ring spin is degrees per second, from −360 to 360. Positive values rotate counterclockwise viewed from above; negative values reverse direction; zero pauses orbiting. Spread sets ring radius. Ring spin can be animated and affects existing particles. Animated position changes move the orbit center.','At 90 degrees per second, a particle completes a circle in four seconds.'],
 ['gravity','Gravity','Motion','Adds acceleration along Z (−100 to 100). Negative values pull downward, zero adds no acceleration, and positive values accelerate upward.','Try −65 with a fountain to make particles arc back down.'],
 ['turbulence','Swirl and turbulence','Motion','Adds changing sideways forces (0–100). Higher values produce more erratic motion. Vortex and Mini galaxy also have a preset-specific circular force.','A small value adds life to smoke without scattering it too far.'],
 ['bounce','Ground bounce','Motion','When enabled, particles bounce off the flat Z=0 plane and lose some speed. This is floor collision only; arbitrary meshes and scene geometry are not collision targets.','Combine negative gravity and bounce for sparks hitting the ground.'],
 ['animation','Particle animation timeline','Animation','Select a layer and open Animate this layer. Add timed property changes or particle events. Each layer has its own animation, saved with the project and included in Niagara exports. Animated layers play once, keeping their last values; use Restart to replay. Up to 64 property keys and 32 events per layer, over 1–60 seconds.','Make a fireball turn red at 1 second, add sparks at 2 seconds, and stop emission at 3 seconds.'],
 ['keyframes','Timed property changes','Animation','Change a property schedules a keyframe. At this time switches the value instantly. Gradually blends from the previous keyframe for that property (or its starting value at time zero). Color, size, opacity, gravity, turbulence, and end-of-life settings affect existing particles. Starting position, speed, spread, and lifetime apply to new particles. Amount controls new particles per second.','Add opacity 0 at time 3 with Gradually to fade the layer out.'],
 ['emissionAnimation','Start and stop new particles','Animation','Animate Emit new particles to Off to stop spawning while existing particles finish their lives. Set it back to On later to resume a continuous flow. Setting Particle amount to zero also stops continuous spawning. Turning emission on in Burst mode does not create another burst; add a timed burst event for that.','Set emission Off at 0 seconds and On at 1 second for a delayed effect.'],
 ['particleEvents','Timed particle bursts and clearing','Animation','Add particles / clear adds an event. A burst creates 1–2000 particles at its scheduled time, even when continuous emission is off. Clear removes all particles already in this layer; it does not disable future spawning. Events fire once per playback. If a clear and burst share a time, clearing happens first.','Clear the layer at 3 seconds and also turn emission Off there to end it completely.'],
 ['scrubbing','Preview an animation time','Animation','Drag Preview time in the playback bar to rebuild all visible layers from the start up to that time. Scrubbing pauses playback. Press Play to continue or Restart to replay from zero. Timeline length controls the scrub range and the latest allowed key or event, not particle lifetime or an automatic stop.','Scrub just before and after a burst time to check when sparks appear.'],
 ['camera','3D camera','Preview','Left-drag to orbit, scroll to zoom, and right-drag to pan. Perspective, Front, Side, and Top are available in the camera menu. The reset button returns home.','Inspect the same effect from several angles before exporting.'],
 ['keyboard','Keyboard controls','Preview','Focus the canvas to use arrow keys to orbit, +/− to zoom, and 0 to reset the camera. Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z undo and redo authoring changes when a text field is not focused. F1 opens documentation.','Camera changes do not alter the saved effect.'],
 ['grid','Ground grid','Preview','The grid button shows or hides the ground plane guide and axis helper. Z is the upward axis. This matches the direction used by gravity and floor bounce.','The grid is a preview guide and is not a spawned particle.'],
 ['background','Preview background','Preview','The background button switches between dark and lighter preview environments. It does not change particles or Unreal assets.','Check bright and dark backgrounds for readability.'],
 ['playback','Playback and restart','Preview','Pause freezes the simulation while allowing camera movement. Restart clears all layer simulations. Speed can be 0.5×, 1×, or 2×. The progress bar shows a repeating ten-second preview cycle.','Pause to inspect particle placement or capture an image.'],
 ['save','Save and open projects','Files','Save project writes an editable .ember.json file. Open project accepts old v1 and layered v2 files. Automatic recovery is local to this app; explicit files are portable.','Keep a project file alongside images and Unreal assets.'],
 ['new','New effect','Files','New effect resets the editor to one Blank particle layer. Undo can restore the previous effect in this session. Save a file first if you need a permanent copy.','Start with Blank particle when learning each control.'],
 ['undo','Undo and redo','Files','Undo restores the previous authoring settings; Redo reapplies them. History is limited to 60 changes in this session. Camera movement and elapsed simulation time are not part of history.','Use toolbar buttons when keyboard shortcuts are unavailable.'],
 ['image','PNG capture','Files','The snapshot button saves the current 3D view as a PNG image. It includes the preview background and visible guides. Hide the grid first for a cleaner capture.','Pause and frame the camera before taking a snapshot.'],
 ['blender','Blender effects and files','Files','Open Blender & files in the top bar. Ember detects Blender or lets you choose blender.exe. Save effect as .blend bakes visible particle layers over 1–20 seconds at 1–30 frames per second. Each layer has its own collection, geometry-node particle shapes, trail geometry, and frame visibility animation. The scene replays after reopening without running scripts. Original Ember settings are embedded in a text block; Open Ember effect from .blend restores those settings. Later manual Blender edits are not converted back into Ember animation keys. Units are converted from Ember centimeters to Blender meters. Large bakes are limited; lower the frame rate or duration if needed.','Export a one-second test at 12 fps first, then open it in Blender and play the timeline.'],
 ['renderer','Imported mesh particles','Appearance','Click Import Blender mesh under Shape & motion, or use Blender & files. Choose a .blend, .glb, or .fbx file. Visible mesh objects are combined as a static particle shape. Use Render particles as to switch between sprites and the imported mesh. Meshes use the layer color and lifetime opacity in Ember; positions, randomness, animation, and trails still work. The local mesh cache persists after closing, and projects and presets remember its reference. Keep the original mesh file for portability; another computer needs it imported again. Each mesh is normalized so particle size remains useful.','Import a small Blender cube, choose Imported 3D mesh, and add random particle angle for varied mesh orientations.'],
 ['meshFiles','Blender and Unreal mesh transfers','Export','Connect an Unreal project first. Send selected mesh to Unreal imports the selected Blender mesh as a new static-mesh asset under Content/Ember/Meshes. Exporting an effect with mesh particles also imports the referenced meshes automatically and creates Niagara mesh renderers. Unreal mesh → Blender lets you pick a static-mesh .uasset inside that project’s Content folder and saves a .blend scene. Close the project in Unreal before a transfer. Skeletal rigs, Blueprint behavior, and Niagara graphs are not translated; transfer is for static mesh geometry and compatible materials. Particle effects are baked for Blender, while their original Ember settings remain available for return to Niagara.','Model a shard in Blender, import it in Ember, then export a burst of mesh particles to the connected Unreal project.'],
 ['connection','Connect an Unreal project','Export','Click Connect Unreal project in the top bar, choose Unreal 5.6 or 5.8, then click Choose project. Select a .uproject file in the file picker. Ember reads the project’s engine version and remembers your selection after closing. Exports go to the connected project, while a final confirmation still tells you what will be created. Use the same button to change projects or disconnect. Connecting does not change any Unreal project files.','Connect your game project once so you can export multiple effects without selecting its file each time.'],
 ['unreal','Unreal export','Export','Choose Unreal 5.6 or 5.8 and a destination .uproject. Close that project in Unreal first. Ember installs its companion editor plugin and creates assets under Content/Ember. Existing names receive a suffix.','Use the exact engine build that matches the bundled plugin.'],
 ['plugin','Editor plugin','Export','Get editor plugin opens the folder for the selected version. Copy EmberNiagara into your project’s Plugins folder, restart Unreal, then choose Tools → Import Ember VFX.','The plugin imports saved project JSON without Python.'],
 ['limits','Supported features and limits','Export','Ember is a standalone sprite and mesh particle authoring app. It does not implement every Niagara feature: arbitrary node scripting, skeletal-mesh sampling, event graphs, skeletal animations, and arbitrary scene collisions are not available.','Use the documented controls for this release; do not expect complete Niagara parity.']
];
function openDocumentation(id='start'){
 dialog('<div class="eyebrow">EMBER DOCUMENTATION · OFFLINE</div><h2>Find your next step.</h2><input id="docs-search" class="search" placeholder="Search features…" aria-label="Search documentation"><div class="docs-layout"><nav id="docs-topics" aria-label="Documentation features"></nav><article id="docs-article"></article></div>');$('#dialog').classList.add('docs-dialog');
 function article(topic){const item=featureDocs.find(d=>d[0]===topic)||featureDocs[0];const host=$('#docs-article');host.replaceChildren();const title=document.createElement('h3');title.textContent=item[1];const body=document.createElement('p');body.textContent=item[3];const tip=document.createElement('p');tip.className='docs-tip';tip.textContent='Try this: '+item[4];host.append(title,body,tip);document.querySelectorAll('[data-doc-topic]').forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('active',b.dataset.docTopic===item[0]));}
 function list(){const query=$('#docs-search').value.toLowerCase();const host=$('#docs-topics');host.replaceChildren();let category='';for(const item of featureDocs.filter(d=>d.join(' ').toLowerCase().includes(query))){if(category!==item[2]){category=item[2];const label=document.createElement('b');label.textContent=category;host.append(label);}const button=document.createElement('button');button.textContent=item[1];button.dataset.docTopic=item[0];button.onclick=()=>article(item[0]);host.append(button);}if(!host.children.length)host.textContent='No matching feature. Try another word.';}
 $('#docs-search').oninput=list;list();article(id);
}
$('#help').textContent='? Documentation';$('#help').onclick=()=>openDocumentation();$('#dialog').addEventListener('close',()=>$('#dialog').classList.remove('docs-dialog'));
const helpTargets={forces:'#dynamics-attraction',surface:'#dynamics-collisionHeight',lifetime:'#dynamics-colorOverLife',anime:'#advanced-anime',renderer:'#particle-renderer',trailLength:'#advanced-trailLength',radiusVariation:'#advanced-radiusVariation',emitScaleX:'#advanced-emitScaleX',emitScaleY:'#advanced-emitScaleY',emitScaleZ:'#advanced-emitScaleZ',spinVariation:'#advanced-spinVariation',directionRandom:'#advanced-directionRandom',spriteRotationRandom:'#advanced-spriteRotationRandom',motion:'#advanced-motion',velocityYaw:'#advanced-velocityYaw',velocityPitch:'#advanced-velocityPitch',ringSpin:'#advanced-ringSpin',positionX:'#advanced-positionX',positionY:'#advanced-positionY',positionZ:'#advanced-positionZ',color:'.colors',amount:'[data-key="amount"]',size:'[data-key="size"]',speed:'[data-key="speed"]',spread:'[data-key="spread"]',life:'[data-key="life"]',mode:'#mode',glow:'#glow',trail:'#trail',shape:'#advanced-shape',sprite:'#advanced-sprite',gravity:'#advanced-gravity',turbulence:'#advanced-turbulence',endSize:'#advanced-endSize',endOpacity:'#advanced-endOpacity',bounce:'#advanced-bounce'};
function attachFeatureHelp(){for(const [id,selector]of Object.entries(helpTargets)){const control=$(selector),label=control?.closest('label');if(!label||label.querySelector('.feature-help'))continue;const button=document.createElement('button');button.className='feature-help';button.type='button';button.textContent='?';button.setAttribute('aria-label','Help for '+(featureDocs.find(d=>d[0]===id)?.[1]||id));button.onclick=e=>{e.preventDefault();openDocumentation(id)};label.append(button);}}
const docsSync=sync;sync=()=>{docsSync();attachFeatureHelp()};attachFeatureHelp();window.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='F1'){e.preventDefault();openDocumentation();}});
